                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\lambda
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\Lambda_\mathcal{C}\left[ (1 \otimes (-)) \right]
                    \\overset{\simeq}{\longrightarrow}
                    (-)
                    \\,.
            %p
          %h3#other_coherence_conditions Other coherence conditions
          %p
            The original list of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+law"} coherence axioms
            for monoidal categories given by Mac Lane in 1963 was longer than what is shown in Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MonoidalCategory"}
            above. But Max Kelly showed they could be whittled down to just the pentagon and triangle identities. We reproduce his arguments here.
          %p
            In the proofs below, monoidal product symbols
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            will be suppressed, to save space.
          #kel1.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a{:href => "#Kelly"}> Kelly 64
                )
              In a monoidal category, the equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi λ
                      %mi x
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi λ
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mi y
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda_x y = \lambda_{x y} \circ \alpha_{1, x, y}
              holds, i.e., the diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
