          %p
            This shows that it is a bit of an illusion to think of a
            %em pentagon
            identity: the full geometric shape is really a 4-dimensional tetrahedron (the 4-simplex) whose five tetrahedral faces are the five edges of the pentagon identity.
          %p
            We can formulate this identification of structure morphisms and coherence laws with orientals more formally using the general notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Desc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Desc(Y,A)
            (constructed in terms of orientals) can be regarded as a way to formalize “lax
            = succeed "-functor" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            to
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            Indeed, using observations pretty much as just sketched, one finds that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            a 2-category that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Desc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi pt
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi WeakMonoids
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Desc(pt, C) \simeq WeakMonoids(C)
          %p
            and for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            the 3-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}Cat
            we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Desc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi pt
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi LaxMonCat
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Desc(pt, \mathbf{B}Cat) \simeq LaxMonCat
                  \\,,
          %p
            where the 2-category on the right is defined as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi MonCat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MonCat
            above, but with the associator not required to be an isomorphism.
          %h2#remark_pseudo_versus_lax_orientals_versus_unorientals Remark: pseudo versus lax, orientals versus unorientals
          %p
            In closing, it should be remarked that the fact that everything here is
            %em lax
            instead of
            %em pseudo
            is related to a curious property of the orientals: the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \ge 1
            %em fails
            to be weakly equivalent to the point. As a result, the objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Desc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi pt
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Desc(pt, C)
            are not quite
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctor"} anafunctor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %em more general
            than ordinary descent, which in its generality is lacking some properties usually required of descent.
          %p
            We can remedy this by replacing in the definition of the descent
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Desc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Desc(Y,C)
            the orientals by another cosimplicial
            = succeed "-category," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %em is
            equivalent to the point in each degree. Doing so and then going through the above discussion will make
            %em all
            the structure maps appeaing have inverses. But this will also apply to the monoidal product itself, then, which is usually not desired.
